.TH velplot "" "Command"
.SH NAME
velplot \- put a vellum drawing on a page
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvelplot\fR [\fB\-T lp\fR|\fBps\fR|\fBhpgl\fR] [\fB\-wide\fR] [\fB\-fit\fR] [\fB\-scale\fR \fIn\fR] \fIfile.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvelplot \-tile\fR [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-T lp\fR|\fBps\fR|\fBhpgl\fR] \fIbig.d ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
velplot -- a vellum drawing onto a page
Usage:	velplot [-T lp|ps|hpgl] [-wide] [-fit] [-scale n] file.d ...
	velplot -tile [-n] [-T lp|ps|hpgl] big.d ...
	-T lp	Epson raster, ESC * bands (the default)
	-T ps	PostScript Level 1, one page per sheet
	-T hpgl	HPGL pen plotter, annotation layer in pen 2
	-wide	landscape: the page turned a quarter (lp)
	-fit	scale the drawing to FILL the page
	-scale n	device dots per grid unit (lp; default 8)
	-tile	as many pages as it takes; -n counts them
.HE
.B velplot
renders drawings made with
.B vellum
onto whatever the shop owns.
It writes to standard output and reads no terminal: the whole
pipeline runs from
.BR make (1),
from a script, or down a serial line on a machine with no bitmap card
at all.
.PP
Each argument is a SHEET, and the file name
.B \-
is standard input, so a generated drawing plots without a temporary
file:
.B "velinfo -symsheet pid.sym | velplot -T ps -fit -" .
Layers marked no-print, and the construction layer, are skipped.
.PP
The device is chosen with
.BR \-T :
.DS
.ta 4n 16n
	\fBlp\fR	Epson raster on stdout \- ESC * 24-row head
		bands, one grid unit = 8 dots by default.  Pipe
		it to \fBlpr\fR.  \fB-wide\fR prints landscape (the page
		turned a quarter) and \fB-scale\fR \fIn\fR picks n dots
		per grid unit, so a small drawing prints big
	\fBps\fR	PostScript (Level 1), one page per sheet \- the
		laser's page, at a pinned 16 grid units to the
		inch.  Text is Courier sized so the monospace
		advance matches the editor cell: nothing collides
		on paper that did not collide on screen.  Smooth
		polylines emit real curveto, vertical text
		rotates, and arcs are true arcs
	\fBhpgl\fR	HPGL pen-plotter commands on stdout.  The
		annotation layer draws in the SECOND pen (SP2) \-
		the cheapest two-color print this machine will
		ever do
.DE
.PP
.B \-fit
scales the drawing to fill the page; for
.B "-T lp"
it picks the widest step of the discrete 2..32 dot ladder instead,
because the head has no fractional dot.
.SH TILING
.B \-tile
is the sheet BIGGER than the paper.
The chosen device runs once per PAGE with the drawing origin stepped,
so a drawing wider than the head comes out as pages that TAPE
TOGETHER: one grid unit of overlap on each seam, crop marks at the
corners and a "2/6 row B col 2" label in the margin, row-major so the
pile is in reading order.
.PP
It composes with
.B \-wide
and
.BR \-scale .
It does NOT compose with
.BR \-fit ,
which means "make this ONE page" and is the opposite instruction;
that combination is an error, not a precedence rule.
.B \-n
prints how many pages it would take and stops.
.PP
Without
.BR \-tile ,
.B "-T lp"
still clips at the head's 960 dots \- but says so on standard error
rather than quietly losing the right-hand edge.
.SH EXAMPLES
.DS
.ta 8n
	velplot amp.d | lpr
	velplot -T ps -fit bracket.d | lpr
	velplot -T hpgl feed.d > /dev/plotter
	velplot -tile -n -T ps big.d
	velplot -tile -T ps big.d | lpr
.DE
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 4n 32n
	/usr/vellum/etc/project.mk	the sheet-set Makefile skeleton,
		which gates \fBprint:\fR on \fBvelcheck\fR and \fBveldiff\fR
	/usr/hr/fonts/*.hf	the glyph cells the raster device
		draws text with
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B vellum ,
.B velpic ,
.B veldxf ,
.B velcheck ,
.B veldiff ,
.B lpr
.SH NOTES
The editor's Print item runs this program on a snapshot of the live
drawing, so what the board prints and what a Makefile prints are the
same bytes.
.PP
The device backends share ONE object walker with the print-preview
window, so what the preview shows is what the paper gets.
